Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "balkan-order":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Balkan Order",eyebrow:"Regional system · Danube, Adriatic, Aegean, and Straits",infoboxKicker:"Contested postwar order · 1947–1985",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/balkan-strategic-board.svg",alt:"Conceptual diagram of German, Italian, Bulgarian, and Turkish positions in the Balkans",caption:"The Balkan board joins the German continental and Italian Mediterranean systems"},
    lead:"The Balkan Order is the political, military, and economic system created by the Axis partition of southeastern Europe and sustained after the German–Italian strategic divorce. It is not a single alliance. German-integrated kingdoms, Italian-associated crowns and protectorates, a balancing Bulgaria, and an armed neutral Turkey meet across railways, river traffic, ports, airfields, minority disputes, and overlapping advisory rights.",
    canon:"The 1947 territorial settlement, enlarged Hungary and Bulgaria, Romanian eastern compensation, the destruction and later limited federal reconstruction of Yugoslavia, Italian primacy in Greece and the Adriatic, differentiated German and Italian client systems, Bulgaria's balancing role, Turkey's armed neutrality, and continuing German–Italian rivalry through 1985 are established. Exact district borders, cabinet sequences, force levels, succession clauses, and the final legal text of the Verona framework remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"definition",title:"A region organized by overlapping systems",html:"<p>The Balkans possess no organization equivalent to the Einheitspakt or Latin Community. The term describes a strategic field in which several unequal systems overlap. Germany approaches through the Danube, railways, industry, oil, and military missions; Italy approaches through crowns, protectorates, ports, customs, bases, and the Mediterranean.</p><p>Local governments retain enough authority to bargain, delay, and play patrons against one another. They do not possess equal freedom to change alignment or reject the settlement that created them.</p>"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"The 1947 territorial settlement",html:"<p>The <a href='danube-balkan-settlement.html'>Danube and Balkan Settlement</a> rewards Axis partners and dismantles the prewar Yugoslav balance. Hungary receives Transylvania and northern Yugoslav districts. Romania receives eastern compensation, Bulgaria gains most of Macedonia, and Italy establishes Adriatic, Greek, and Aegean primacy.</p><p>The arrangement creates minorities, exposed corridors, divided transport systems, and dissatisfied clients. Dependence is therefore not an unintended flaw. It is one of the settlement's principal stabilizing mechanisms.</p>"},
      {id:"german",title:"The German continental system",html:"<p><a href='hungary.html'>Hungary</a> and <a href='romania.html'>Romania</a> remain nominally independent while their currencies, heavy industry, procurement, railways, and security planning are integrated with the Einheitspakt. Austria, no longer sovereign, forms Germany's internal southeastern gateway through <a href='vienna.html'>Vienna</a>.</p><p>German influence follows connected infrastructure rather than requiring formal annexation. Staff colleges, technical missions, credit, energy, and standardized equipment can constrain a ministry as effectively as a treaty garrison.</p>"},
      {id:"italian",title:"The Italian Adriatic and Aegean system",html:"<p>Italy governs through differentiated forms rather than one colonial statute. The <a href='italian-union-of-crowns.html'>Union of Crowns</a> associates Greece and the <a href='federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html'>Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> with Umberto II, while Albania, Serbia, Montenegro, and other territories occupy distinct constitutional positions.</p><p>Local ministries, flags, courts, churches, and armed formations coexist with Italian control of external defense, strategic bases, treaty alignment, and upper command. The <a href='umbertian-opening.html'>Umbertian Opening</a> makes dependence more governable without making it equal.</p>"},
      {id:"bulgaria",title:"Bulgaria, the penetrated hinge",html:"<p><a href='bulgaria.html'>Bulgaria</a> is formally sovereign and belongs fully to neither system. German rail, industry, radar, armor, and officer education meet Italian access to Aegean ports, training, credit, and diplomatic protection.</p><p>The <a href='balkan-missions-crisis.html'>Balkan Missions Crisis</a> and <a href='sofia-procurement-crisis.html'>Sofia Procurement Crisis</a> demonstrate the Bulgarian method: accept enough from each patron to prevent monopoly, then turn the threatened alternative into better terms. The method preserves choice while deepening both penetrations.</p>"},
      {id:"turkey",title:"Turkey and the armed gate",html:"<p><a href='turkey.html'>Turkey</a> guards the Straits and the land bridge between Europe, the Black Sea, and the Middle East. It does not enter either bloc, and its military readiness makes foreign access a negotiation rather than an assumed right.</p><p>The <a href='straits-security-settlement.html'>Straits Security Settlement</a>, selective mobilization, inspection, and carefully distributed commercial relations allow Ankara to maintain balance. Any German–Italian Balkan crisis therefore produces a Turkish calculation as well as a local one.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Railways, rivers, ports, and procurement",html:"<p>The regional order is material before it is ceremonial. Danube shipping, Belgrade and Sofia rail junctions, Romanian oil, Adriatic harbors, Aegean bases, pipelines, electrical grids, and communications standards determine which foreign power can move, supply, and observe.</p><p>Procurement has constitutional weight. An aircraft, radar net, locomotive standard, or encrypted staff circuit can bring instructors, spares, doctrine, and continuing access. Contracts therefore become diplomatic instruments and recurring causes of crisis.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"National claims, minorities, and churches",html:"<p>Territorial rewards leave Hungarians, Romanians, South Slavs, Greeks, Bulgarians, Albanians, Turks, and others under governments they do not necessarily identify as their own. Border revision has settled frontiers more readily than loyalties.</p><p>Orthodox, Catholic, Eastern Catholic, Muslim, and Jewish institutions cross or complicate state boundaries. Germany and Italy invoke protection selectively, while local rulers use schools, dioceses, property, language, and military service to turn postwar possession into durable authority.</p>"},
      {id:"rivalry",title:"From Axis partnership to managed rivalry",html:"<p>The deaths of Hitler and Mussolini remove the personal structure that contained competing continental and Mediterranean ambitions. The <a href='german-italian-rivalry.html'>German–Italian Strategic Rivalry</a> becomes visible through advisers, exercises, intelligence, railway rights, and client procurement rather than a declared break.</p><p>Crisis rules gradually limit exclusive bases, cap missions, require notification, and separate equipment sales from operational control. These practices reduce surprise; they do not reconcile the rival maps beneath them.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"The order by 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the Balkan system is durable because every major participant fears the cost of overturning it. Germany can penetrate but not safely absorb the Italian sphere. Italy can preserve crowns and bases but cannot sever continental commerce. Bulgaria can balance but cannot exclude both patrons. Turkey can deny passage but cannot ignore the surrounding blocs.</p><p>The result is neither peace between equals nor permanent prewar occupation. It is a mature hierarchy maintained through local administration, external guarantees, economic dependence, and repeated negotiation at the edge of military crisis.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"danube-balkan-settlement.html",kicker:"Territorial foundation",label:"Danube and Balkan Settlement"},{href:"german-italian-rivalry.html",kicker:"Great-power relationship",label:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry"},{href:"italian-union-of-crowns.html",kicker:"Mediterranean system",label:"Italian Union of Crowns"},{href:"bulgaria.html",kicker:"Balancing state",label:"Kingdom of Bulgaria"},{href:"turkey.html",kicker:"Armed neutral",label:"Republic of Turkey"},{href:"austria.html",kicker:"German gateway",label:"Austria within Greater Germany"}],
    facts:[["Formative settlement","1947"],["Principal external powers","Greater German Reich and Kingdom of Italy"],["German-associated states","Hungary and Romania"],["Italian-associated system","Greece, Federal Yugoslavia, and Adriatic protectorates"],["Balancing hinge","Kingdom of Bulgaria"],["Armed neutral gate","Republic of Turkey"],["Principal instruments","Rail, river, ports, bases, missions, credit, and procurement"],["Status by 1985","Durable managed rivalry"]]
  }),

  "danube-balkan-settlement":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Danube and Balkan Settlement",eyebrow:"Postwar territorial order · from 1947",infoboxKicker:"European frontier settlement",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/balkan-strategic-board.svg",alt:"Conceptual diagram of the Balkan strategic board",caption:"German continental and Italian Mediterranean systems meet across the Balkan board"},
    lead:"The Danube and Balkan Settlement was the territorial and strategic order imposed after the Axis victory. It abolished Yugoslavia, enlarged Hungary and Bulgaria, compensated Romania eastward, placed Greece and much of the southern Balkans beneath Italy, and left the region divided so that nearly every client required an external guarantor.",
    canon:"The partition of Yugoslavia, Hungarian possession of all Transylvania and northern Yugoslav gains, Romanian eastern compensation, Bulgarian Macedonia, Italian Greece and Adriatic dominance, and the region's deliberate instability are established. Several district boundaries and the earliest client constitutions remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"principle",title:"A settlement designed against independence",html:"<p>The settlement does not create a stable family of sovereign nation-states. Berlin and Rome distribute rewards, punishments, corridors, and protectorates so that no regional power can reverse the peace without assistance from one of the two victorious empires.</p>"},
      {id:"hungary",title:"Greater Hungary and the Transylvanian lever",html:"<p><a href='hungary.html'>Hungary</a> receives all Transylvania, northern former Yugoslav districts, and the Slovak lands. The enlarged kingdom gains territory and prestige but also non-Hungarian populations, long frontiers, and military obligations that make German economic and security integration difficult to escape.</p>"},
      {id:"romania",title:"Romania's eastern compensation",html:"<p><a href='romania.html'>Romania</a> loses Transylvania while receiving eastern territory and influence around Odessa. Oil, railways, Black Sea access, and German forces matter more than the formal sovereignty of Bucharest. The Hungarian–Romanian grievance remains a permanent German instrument.</p>"},
      {id:"yugoslavia",title:"The destruction of Yugoslavia",html:"<p>The prewar kingdom disappears. Hungary takes Vojvodina, Bačka, Baranja, and other northern districts; Bulgaria takes most of Macedonia; Italy controls Dalmatia and the Adriatic; Slovenia is divided; and Serbia, Croatia, Bosnia, and Montenegro enter separate protected arrangements.</p>"},
      {id:"italy",title:"Italian Adriatic and Aegean primacy",html:"<p>Italy dominates Greece, Albania, Dalmatia, Montenegro, the coast, and the approaches to the eastern Mediterranean. Its power is strongest at ports, bases, customs houses, and client capitals, but German rail and commercial rights continue through the interior.</p>"},
      {id:"bulgaria",title:"Bulgaria as the unresolved hinge",html:"<p><a href='bulgaria.html'>Bulgaria</a> gains Macedonia and access to both continental and Aegean systems. Neither Germany nor Italy concedes it wholly to the other. Sofia turns this ambiguity into loans, weapons, routes, and bargaining space.</p>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"From occupation to differentiated association",html:"<p>Direct occupation gradually yields to protectorates, crowned dependencies, and the <a href='federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html'>Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a>. Local ministries administer schools, courts, budgets, roads, and ordinary policing while defense, foreign alignment, heavy arms, and strategic transport remain externally controlled.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"The Balkan board after the Axis divorce",html:"<p>Once Germany and Italy become strategic rivals, every ambiguous right becomes a potential crisis. Bulgarian advisers, Serbian railways, Greek airfields, Turkish passage, Romanian exercises, and Yugoslav constitutional claims turn the 1947 settlement into a continuing diplomatic system rather than a concluded peace.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"german-italian-rivalry.html",kicker:"Great-power system",label:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry"},{href:"federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html",kicker:"Southern successor",label:"Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia"},{href:"bulgaria.html",kicker:"Strategic hinge",label:"Kingdom of Bulgaria"},{href:"turkey.html",kicker:"Armed gate",label:"Republic of Turkey"}],
    facts:[["Effective from","1947"],["Principal powers","Greater German Reich and Kingdom of Italy"],["Abolished state","Kingdom of Yugoslavia"],["Hungarian gain","All Transylvania and northern Yugoslav districts"],["Bulgarian gain","Most of Macedonia"],["Italian zone","Adriatic, Greece, and Aegean approaches"],["Political effect","Dependent and mutually dissatisfied clients"],["Status by 1985","Still operative beneath later conventions"]]
  }),

  "german-italian-rivalry":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry",eyebrow:"Axis divorce · c. 1961 onward",infoboxKicker:"Managed inter-bloc rivalry",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/balkan-strategic-board.svg",alt:"Conceptual diagram of the principal Balkan theaters",caption:"Bulgaria, Serbia, and Turkey become the most exposed European theaters"},
    lead:"The German–Italian Strategic Rivalry was the managed rupture that replaced the wartime Axis relationship after the deaths of Mussolini and Hitler. Neither government formally repudiated the alliance tradition; both quietly ended joint planning, armed opposing clients, contested technical missions, and prepared for the possibility that the former partner would stand on the other side of a European war.",
    canon:"The structural split, its gradual emergence between 1961 and 1965, the Balkans, the two Frances, Iraq, Catholic politics, Latin America, and technical competition as principal theaters, and the personal restraining relationship between Umberto II and Wolfgang are established. The Verona Convention's late-1985 adoption, working name, and core restraints are established; its exact signature date, complete legal text, and implementation details remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"The exhaustion of the Axis partnership",html:"<p>Hitler and Mussolini could preserve a personal and historical partnership even while interests diverged. Their deaths remove the last authority able to treat Italian Mediterranean and German continental ambitions as parts of one revolutionary war.</p>"},
      {id:"divorce",title:"A cold divorce",html:"<p>Joint plans lapse, intelligence collection expands, industrial standards diverge, and exercises begin to assume Italian neutrality or hostility. Public statements continue to describe historic partners with regional differences because admitting the Axis has ended would expose both regimes to uncomfortable questions of legitimacy.</p>"},
      {id:"balkans",title:"The Balkan board",html:"<p><a href='bulgaria.html'>Bulgaria</a> is the penetrated hinge, <a href='italian-protectorate-serbia.html'>Serbia</a> the railway dispute, and <a href='turkey.html'>Turkey</a> the armed gate. Germany seeks connected continental infrastructure; Italy insists that commerce cannot create political rights inside its protected system.</p>"},
      {id:"france",title:"The two Frances",html:"<p>Northern France and Occitania become armed mirrors. Berlin and Rome permit dependent rearmament, intelligence competition, border exercises, and rival claims to French legitimacy without permitting either client to fight a war that might draw the great powers in directly.</p>"},
      {id:"iraq",title:"From missions to proxy war",html:"<p>The Iraqi Civil War transforms rivalry from advisers and railway notes into clients killing one another. Germany supports the Arab National Revolutionary Command while Italy and Britain sustain the Hashemite royal government through Jordan and Syria.</p>"},
      {id:"rulers",title:"Umberto and Wolfgang",html:"<p>Umberto treats Wolfgang as a sovereign rather than merely Hitler's son, while Wolfgang values the candor of an older monarch. Their direct channel restrains deployments and saves face, yet neither mistakes personal warmth for common strategy.</p>"},
      {id:"rules",title:"The proto-Verona practices",html:"<p>Exercise notification, adviser ceilings, limits on exclusive basing, emergency staff talks, and separation of equipment sales from operational control emerge before any formal convention. Practice precedes law because both powers need restraint before they can admit why.</p>"},
      {id:"balance",title:"Rivalry after Verona",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-h6v2q writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T17:25:00-06:00 --><p>In late 1985 the <a href='verona-convention.html'>Verona Convention</a> formalizes non-aggression, exercise notification, emergency consultation, and restraint of client aggression while leaving Bulgaria unresolved. The two systems continue to trade, exchange diplomats, and compete in the Mediterranean, Africa, France, the Balkans, and space.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"balkan-missions-crisis.html",kicker:"First confrontation",label:"Balkan Missions Crisis"},{href:"sofia-procurement-crisis.html",kicker:"1966 precedent",label:"Sofia Procurement Crisis"},{href:"italy.html",kicker:"Latin power",label:"Kingdom of Italy"},{href:"germany.html",kicker:"Continental power",label:"Greater German Reich"}],
    facts:[["Period","c. 1961 onward"],["Predecessor relationship","Wartime Axis partnership"],["Character","Managed strategic divorce"],["Principal European theaters","Balkans and the two Frances"],["First proxy war","Iraqi Civil War"],["Key interlocutors","Umberto II and Wolfgang Hitler"],["Early restraints","Adviser ceilings and exercise notification"],["Later framework","Verona Convention, late 1985; core terms established"]]
  }),

  "balkan-missions-crisis":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Balkan Missions Crisis",eyebrow:"German–Italian confrontation · c. 1962",infoboxKicker:"Advisory-rights crisis",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/balkan-strategic-board.svg",alt:"Balkan strategic board during the missions crisis",caption:"Competing missions made overlapping German and Italian maps visible"},
    lead:"The Balkan Missions Crisis was the first open post-Axis confrontation over whether German or Italian advisers possessed legitimate authority in Bulgaria and Serbia. It did not begin with an invasion or territorial claim. It began with appointments, training rights, railway missions, communications access, and the proposition that a technical presence might carry sovereign consequences.",
    canon:"A Bulgarian command reorganization around 1962, Italian demands, German refusal, exercises north of the Danube, Italian readiness in Greece and the Aegean, Turkish precautionary mobilization, and a personal Göring–Umberto compromise are established as a framework. Exact dates, named ministers, and orders of battle remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Advisers after victory",html:"<p>German and Italian missions had entered the Balkans as wartime partners. After peace, their railway engineers, officers, intelligence liaisons, lenders, and industrial representatives remained inside states whose formal sovereignty did not decide which foreign institution held practical authority.</p>"},
      {id:"appointments",title:"The Bulgarian command appointments",html:"<p>A royal ministry shifts several senior posts from Italian-oriented officers toward German-trained professionals. Sofia presents an internal reorganization; Berlin calls it modernization; Rome sees a quiet seizure of the Bulgarian command structure.</p>"},
      {id:"demands",title:"Italian demands",html:"<p>Rome demands limits on the German mission, guarantees against air and intelligence bases, preservation of Italian training rights, and consultation before future appointments. The demands would make Italy a co-author of Bulgarian military sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"refusal",title:"German refusal",html:"<p>Berlin refuses to recognize Bulgaria as an exclusively Italian sphere. German officials argue that continental rail, Black Sea defense, Romanian oil, and approaches to the Caucasus require a professional Bulgarian staff tied to German systems.</p>"},
      {id:"movements",title:"Readiness without mobilization",html:"<p>German exercises occur north of the Danube; Italian naval and air forces raise readiness in Greece and the Aegean; Turkey quietly activates selected formations. Neither power mobilizes nationally, allowing every movement to be described as routine.</p>"},
      {id:"compromise",title:"The Göring–Umberto formula",html:"<p>Personal intervention produces a temporary arrangement: no exclusive bases, continued German economic and railway rights, continued Italian training and Aegean access, nominally sovereign Bulgarian appointments, and no serious attempt to expel either intelligence service.</p>"},
      {id:"serbia",title:"The Serbian remainder",html:"<p>The crisis does not resolve German rights in Italian-protected Serbia. Railways, Danube traffic, concessions, and liaison officers continue to create daily disputes too technical for dramatic settlement and too strategically important to ignore.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"From temporary formula to permanent method",html:"<p>The compromise establishes the method later refined during the <a href='sofia-procurement-crisis.html'>Sofia Procurement Crisis</a>: limit foreign personnel and operational control without forbidding equipment, commerce, or national appointments. Bulgaria's independence becomes the balance between penetrations.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"bulgaria.html",kicker:"Principal theater",label:"Kingdom of Bulgaria"},{href:"italian-protectorate-serbia.html",kicker:"Continuing dispute",label:"Italian Protectorate of Serbia"},{href:"german-italian-rivalry.html",kicker:"Wider system",label:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry"},{href:"sofia-procurement-crisis.html",kicker:"Later confrontation",label:"Sofia Procurement Crisis"}],
    facts:[["Working date","c. 1962"],["Cause","Bulgarian command appointments and advisory rights"],["German posture","Exercises north of the Danube"],["Italian posture","Readiness in Greece and the Aegean"],["Third-state reaction","Selective Turkish mobilization"],["Mediators","Hermann Göring and Umberto II"],["Immediate result","No exclusive Bulgarian basing"],["Long-term result","Permanent managed rivalry"]]
  }),

  "bulgaria":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Kingdom of Bulgaria",category:"Countries",eyebrow:"Sovereign hinge · German–Latin balance",infoboxKicker:"Balkan kingdom · 1985",
    flag:{src:"assets/flags/bulgaria.svg",alt:"White, green, and red flag of Bulgaria",caption:"Bulgarian national tricolor"},
    lead:"The Kingdom of Bulgaria is the formally sovereign hinge between the German continental and Italian-led Mediterranean systems. Territorial gains in Macedonia make the postwar kingdom larger, while competing missions, loans, railways, ports, weapons, and officer networks make its independence inseparable from the refusal of either great power to permit the other exclusive control.",
    canon:"Monarchical continuity, Macedonian gains, formal independence, German and Italian penetration, the Balkan Missions and Sofia procurement crises, German equipment under Bulgarian command, and balancing through 1985 are established. The sovereign, cabinet sequence, precise Macedonian border, and party constitution remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"settlement",title:"The victorious territorial settlement",html:"<p>Bulgaria emerges from the war with most of Macedonia and a stronger position than the defeated Yugoslav state. The gain brings strategic depth, new minorities, disputed churches and schools, and a direct frontier with Italian-ruled Greece.</p>"},
      {id:"crown",title:"Crown and ministries",html:"<p>The monarchy, ministries, Orthodox Church, Army, and civil service retain national form. Sovereignty is real enough to make appointments and contracts contested, but too constrained to exclude both patrons or redirect the state against their combined opposition.</p>"},
      {id:"germany",title:"The German offer",html:"<p>Germany supplies railway electrification, heavy machinery, Romanian oil, Black Sea trade, radar, air defense, officer education, aircraft, and armor. German-trained staff officers and contract-bound ministries view continental integration as modernization rather than surrender.</p>"},
      {id:"latin",title:"The Latin offer",html:"<p>Italy and the Latin Community provide Aegean access, Thessaloniki privileges, development credit, agricultural markets, electrical and consumer investment, naval cooperation, and diplomatic help in selected Macedonian disputes.</p>"},
      {id:"auction",title:"The auction-house strategy",html:"<p>Sofia rarely refuses a foreign system outright. It accepts enough of each offer to prevent exclusion, then uses the threatened alternative to improve price, access, training, and political guarantees. Success deepens foreign penetration even as it preserves formal choice.</p>"},
      {id:"officers",title:"The divided officer corps",html:"<p>German-trained staff officers coexist with Italian-trained air and naval officers, royal conservatives, Orthodox nationalists, and commanders rooted in Macedonian security. Procurement choices therefore rearrange domestic factions as well as weapons inventories.</p>"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"The two Sofia crises",html:"<p>The c. 1962 missions dispute concerns appointments and advisory rights. The <a href='sofia-procurement-crisis.html'>1966 procurement crisis</a> concerns whether integrated radar and communications would give Germany operational control. Both end with ceilings rather than exclusion.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Position by 1985",html:"<p>Bulgaria belongs fully to neither bloc. Its rail and industrial economy leans north; its Aegean access and diplomatic insurance lean south. Foreign maps often color it according to the cartographer's thesis, while Bulgarian doctrine treats ambiguous alignment as a national asset.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"danube-balkan-settlement.html",kicker:"Founding order",label:"Danube and Balkan Settlement"},{href:"balkan-missions-crisis.html",kicker:"Early crisis",label:"Balkan Missions Crisis"},{href:"sofia-procurement-crisis.html",kicker:"1966 confrontation",label:"Sofia Procurement Crisis"},{href:"straits-security-settlement.html",kicker:"Southern comparison",label:"Straits Security Settlement"}],
    facts:[["Capital","Sofia"],["Government","Monarchy; exact cabinet system open"],["Alignment","Balanced between German and Latin systems"],["Principal postwar gain","Most of Macedonia"],["Northern connection","Danube, Romania, and German continental rail"],["Southern connection","Greece, Thessaloniki, and the Aegean"],["Defense principle","Bulgarian command over mixed foreign equipment"],["Strategic description","The penetrated hinge"]]
  }),

  "sofia-procurement-crisis":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Sofia Procurement Crisis",eyebrow:"Bulgarian air-defense confrontation · 1966",infoboxKicker:"Technical-mission crisis",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/sofia-procurement-system.svg",alt:"Diagram of competing German and Latin procurement offers",caption:"Bulgaria accepted German equipment while denying foreign operational command"},
    lead:"The Sofia Procurement Crisis of 1966 was a German–Italian confrontation over an integrated Bulgarian air-defense and signals package. The dispute established that radars, communications, advisers, software, and command procedures could amount to a strategic base even when no foreign combat formation was permanently stationed on Bulgarian soil.",
    canon:"The German package, Italian and Occitan counteroffer, exercises in Romania and Greek Macedonia, direct Umberto–Wolfgang communication, adviser and control ceilings, Bulgarian purchase of German equipment, and Latin counterweight are established. Manufacturers, quantities, exact dates, and named commanders remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"proposal",title:"The German proposal",html:"<p>Germany offers linked radar, interceptors, communications, identification procedures, training, and advisers as one Black Sea defense system. Its military value depends precisely upon integration rather than the sale of separate objects.</p>"},
      {id:"italian-view",title:"The Italian interpretation",html:"<p>Rome argues that the command architecture would let German officers see Greek air activity, shape Bulgarian readiness, and influence decisions near the Straits. A mission able to define the recognized air picture possesses a base's strategic effects without its flag.</p>"},
      {id:"counteroffer",title:"The Latin counteroffer",html:"<p>Italy and Occitania assemble an alternative radar package, development loans, expanded Thessaloniki access, and mixed procurement. The offer is deliberately less unified: technical duplication is the political price of preventing German monopoly.</p>"},
      {id:"deployments",title:"Counterdeployments",html:"<p>Germany exercises formations in Romania. Italy holds exercises in Greek Macedonia and reinforces northern Greek airfields. Neither side mobilizes nationally, and both leave the other's ruler room to describe restraint as voluntary.</p>"},
      {id:"channel",title:"The Umberto–Wolfgang channel",html:"<p>Direct communication prevents staff assumptions from becoming automatic deployments. Wolfgang insists that equipment sales do not erase Bulgarian sovereignty; Umberto insists that a foreign-controlled network would make the sovereignty merely decorative.</p>"},
      {id:"terms",title:"The Sofia compromise",html:"<p>Adviser numbers and tours are limited; foreign crews cannot operate a permanent combat force; the command network remains legally and operationally Bulgarian; and foreign access to data and facilities is bounded by agreement.</p>"},
      {id:"procurement",title:"A deliberately mixed system",html:"<p>Bulgaria buys the German core but purchases selected Italian or Occitan systems and retains national operators. The resulting force is less technically elegant than Berlin proposed and more politically survivable than Rome feared.</p>"},
      {id:"precedent",title:"The technical-base precedent",html:"<p>The crisis creates a rule later used throughout the proto-Verona system: ownership of equipment, presence of advisers, access to data, and authority to issue operational orders are different legal questions. A state may accept the first two while reserving the latter two.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"bulgaria.html",kicker:"Host state",label:"Kingdom of Bulgaria"},{href:"balkan-missions-crisis.html",kicker:"Earlier dispute",label:"Balkan Missions Crisis"},{href:"german-italian-rivalry.html",kicker:"Strategic context",label:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry"},{href:"straits-security-settlement.html",kicker:"Related restraint system",label:"Straits Security Settlement"}],
    facts:[["Date","1966"],["Object of dispute","Integrated Bulgarian air-defense and signals system"],["German argument","Defense of the Black Sea flank"],["Italian argument","Disguised permanent military presence"],["German counterdeployment","Exercises in Romania"],["Italian counterdeployment","Exercises and reinforced airfields in Greece"],["Settlement","German equipment under Bulgarian command"],["Doctrinal result","A technical mission may equal a base"]]
  }),

  "italian-protectorate-serbia":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Italian Protectorate of Serbia",category:"Countries",eyebrow:"Protected Serbian state · 1947–c. 1962",infoboxKicker:"South-Slav protectorate",
    flag:{src:"assets/flags/serbia.svg",alt:"Red, blue, and white Serbian tricolor",caption:"Serbian tricolor retained by the protectorate administration"},
    lead:"The Italian Protectorate of Serbia was the reduced Serbian state created from the southern remnant of occupied Yugoslavia. Italy held formal political and security primacy, while German railways, Danube traffic, industrial concessions, communications, and liaison networks made the protectorate the classic case of Italian sovereignty resting upon German infrastructure.",
    canon:"A reduced Serbia centered on Belgrade, Morava, Kosovo, and the Leskovac region; Italian protection; German railway and commercial rights; continuing nationalist institutions; and later incorporation into Federal Yugoslavia are established. The exact government, borders, and federal transition date remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"partition",title:"Serbia after Yugoslavia",html:"<p>Hungary takes the northern districts, Bulgaria takes most of Macedonia, Italy controls the Adriatic, and neighboring protectorates separate from Belgrade. Serbia retains the capital, Morava corridor, Kosovo, and a reduced southern interior.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Protected government",html:"<p>Serbian ministries, courts, police, municipalities, and Orthodox institutions survive beneath Italian reserve powers. Rome controls external security, alignment, senior military assistance, and the conditions under which the state may arm.</p>"},
      {id:"italy",title:"Italian political primacy",html:"<p>Italy presents protection as restoration of Serbian administration after wartime collapse. Garrisons, advisers, police agreements, and access to Greek and Adriatic bases give Rome the visible instruments of sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"germany",title:"German plumbing",html:"<p>Germany retains railway rights, Danube traffic, industrial concessions, communications facilities, military liaisons, and ties to officers and businessmen. The continental system cannot reach the southern Balkans cleanly without Serbian passage.</p>"},
      {id:"dispute",title:"Infrastructure as a sovereignty dispute",html:"<p>Rome argues that German commerce cannot confer political authority. Berlin argues that Italy cannot interrupt infrastructure upon which several German-aligned economies depend. Routine schedules and technical standards therefore acquire diplomatic weight.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"Serbian society and resistance",html:"<p>Orthodox parishes, veterans, peasant communities, municipal notables, officials, and nationalist networks rebuild within narrow limits. Some seek accommodation, some use German access against Italian officials, and others reject both foreign systems.</p>"},
      {id:"federal",title:"Entry into Federal Yugoslavia",html:"<p>The <a href='umbertian-opening.html'>Umbertian opening</a> places Serbia beneath a federal Yugoslav crown with constituent institutions. The change lowers routine Italian policing costs without returning lost territory or giving Belgrade independent foreign policy.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy of the protectorate",html:"<p>Serbian officials enter the federation with stronger national institutions than the federal center. The old Italian–German railway dispute survives inside federal ministries, where every claim to Yugoslav coordination can also be read as a Serbian attempt to recover primacy.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html",kicker:"Successor system",label:"Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia"},{href:"danube-balkan-settlement.html",kicker:"Founding partition",label:"Danube and Balkan Settlement"},{href:"balkan-missions-crisis.html",kicker:"Foreign missions",label:"Balkan Missions Crisis"},{href:"montenegro.html",kicker:"Western neighbor",label:"Kingdom of Montenegro"}],
    facts:[["Capital","Belgrade"],["Period","1947–c. 1962; transition date open"],["Legal status","Italian protectorate"],["Retained core","Belgrade, Morava, Kosovo, and southern Serbia"],["Northern losses","Hungarian sphere"],["Southern loss","Macedonia to Bulgaria"],["German reserved interest","Rail, Danube, commerce, and communications"],["Successor","Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia"]]
  }),

  "federal-kingdom-yugoslavia":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia",category:"Countries",eyebrow:"Composite kingdom · Italian strategic association",infoboxKicker:"Federal South-Slav kingdom",
    flag:{src:"assets/flags/federal-yugoslavia.svg",alt:"Blue, white, and red flag of Federal Yugoslavia",caption:"Royal Yugoslav tricolor used by the federal kingdom"},
    lead:"The Federal Kingdom of Yugoslavia is the smaller South-Slav federation created during the Umbertian opening from Italy's Serbian, Croatian, Bosnian, Montenegrin, and related protected administrations. It has constituent governments, a federal assembly, a common internal market, and a limited army, while Italy retains external defense, arms supply, bases, customs alignment, and foreign policy.",
    canon:"The federal restoration, reduced territory, constituent institutions, federal cabinet and assembly, common market, limited heavy weapons, Umberto's crown or king-protector role, and Italian reserve powers are established. The exact title, constitution, capital settlement, constituent borders, and succession after Umberto remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"restoration",title:"A restoration smaller than the old kingdom",html:"<p>The federation does not reverse the <a href='danube-balkan-settlement.html'>1947 partition</a>. Italian Dalmatia, Hungarian acquisitions, German zones, and Bulgarian Macedonia remain outside. Restored Yugoslavia covers only the South-Slav territories inside Italy's protected system.</p>"},
      {id:"constituents",title:"Constituent governments",html:"<p>Serbia, Croatia, Bosnia, Montenegro, and supervised portions of Slovenia or Macedonia where applicable maintain separate administrations. Schools, religion, policing, budgets, courts, and local representation are not simply transferred to Belgrade.</p>"},
      {id:"federal",title:"Federal institutions",html:"<p>A small cabinet and an assembly divided between territorial and corporate representation manage the internal market, interregional transport, fiscal disputes, and common services. Federal weakness is intentional: Rome prefers arbitration to a new dominant center.</p>"},
      {id:"crown",title:"Umberto's Yugoslav crown",html:"<p>Umberto II serves as king or king-protector through a crown legally distinct from Italy. The monarchy supplies an arbiter above Serbian and Croatian claims while making the federal state inseparable from the Italian dynastic system.</p>"},
      {id:"reserve",title:"Italian reserved powers",html:"<p>Italy controls the defense treaty, senior advisers, heavy arms, foreign relations, customs alignment, and strategic bases. Yugoslav forces can police territory and conduct limited defense but cannot build an independent great-power military.</p>"},
      {id:"religion",title:"Churches and national patrimonies",html:"<p>Catholic Croatian and Slovenian institutions, Serbian and Montenegrin Orthodoxy, Bosnian Islam, and other communities make confessional uniformity impossible. The Council of Christ the King's language of ecclesial patrimony gives the crown a doctrine for protecting difference without granting equality of sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Internal market and Italian alignment",html:"<p>Federal customs, roads, river traffic, labor movement, and development budgets reduce the cost of five protected economies. External trade and strategic investment remain aligned to Rome, while German rail access through Serbia complicates every claim to exclusive Italian control.</p>"},
      {id:"danger",title:"The constitutional danger",html:"<p>The federation solves an occupation problem by creating institutions able to demand more. Officials can argue for a Yugoslav foreign policy, recovery of Dalmatia or northern districts, heavier forces, or genuine dynastic succession. Rome must preserve enough federation to govern cheaply without allowing it to become sovereign.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"belgrade.html",kicker:"Federal seat",label:"Belgrade"},{href:"italian-union-of-crowns.html",kicker:"Imperial constitution",label:"Italian Union of Crowns"},{href:"italian-protectorate-serbia.html",kicker:"Predecessor state",label:"Italian Protectorate of Serbia"},{href:"umbertian-opening.html",kicker:"Founding reform",label:"Umbertian Opening"},{href:"montenegro.html",kicker:"Constituent monarchy",label:"Montenegro"},{href:"capitals-of-latin-system.html",kicker:"Regional comparison",label:"Capital Cities of the Latin System"}],
    facts:[["Formation","c. 1961–62; exact act open"],["Crown","Umberto II as king or king-protector"],["Principal constituents","Serbia, Croatia, Bosnia, and Montenegro"],["Excluded territories","Italian Dalmatia, Hungarian and German gains, Bulgarian Macedonia"],["Legislature","Territorial and corporate federal assembly"],["Armed forces","Limited Yugoslav army"],["Strategic controller","Kingdom of Italy"],["Succession after Umberto","Open"]]
  }),

  "umbertian-opening":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Umbertian Opening",eyebrow:"Imperial constitutional reforms · 1960–1962",infoboxKicker:"Post-Mussolini imperial reform",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/italian-union-of-crowns.svg",alt:"Diagram of the Umbertian union of crowns",caption:"Separate domestic governments assumed expensive functions beneath Italian strategic control"},
    lead:"The Umbertian Opening was the constitutional reorganization through which Umberto II converted direct Italian occupations into a union of crowns, protectorates, and associated kingdoms. Greece and Federal Yugoslavia received separate governments, while Albania, Ethiopia, Egypt, and Syria occupied different legal layers beneath common Italian strategic supremacy.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-r8k3v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T20:31:00-06:00 -->Umberto's practical control around 1960–61, Mussolini's death or loss of practical authority within that open date range, his non-replacement as Duce, the transfer of ordinary administration downward, separate Greek and Yugoslav crowns, and continuing Italian control of defense, foreign alignment, bases, customs, and strategic finance are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Mussolini's exact death or retirement date and the precise enactment dates and instruments remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"succession",title:"From Duce to royal permanence",html:"<p>Mussolini dies publicly and is absorbed into national memory rather than repudiated. The Fascist Council does not appoint another Duce. Umberto makes the revolutionary founder part of a royal history whose institutions can continue without a second charismatic dictator.</p>"},
      {id:"problem",title:"The cost of direct empire",html:"<p>Italy possesses more territories than its ministries and garrisons can administer efficiently. Schools, local roads, courts, welfare, budgets, and routine policing consume metropolitan capacity without necessarily strengthening strategic control.</p>"},
      {id:"principle",title:"Loosen direct rule to preserve supremacy",html:"<p>The reform transfers expensive ordinary functions to native governments while retaining the powers that determine war, alignment, customs, currency relations, strategic concessions, and basing. Subject governments experience real administrative enlargement without a right of exit.</p>"},
      {id:"greece",title:"The Greek conquest crown",html:"<p>The <a href='act-of-crown-association-greece.html'>Act of Crown Association</a> restores a legally separate Greek kingdom under Umberto. Greek language, courts, treasury, cabinet, forces, and Orthodox establishment return beneath Italian defense and treaty authority.</p>"},
      {id:"yugoslavia",title:"Federal Yugoslavia",html:"<p>Serbian, Croatian, Bosnian, Montenegrin, and other protected administrations enter a smaller <a href='federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html'>federal kingdom</a>. Rome gains one treaty and one crown while local governments assume routine administration.</p>"},
      {id:"other-realms",title:"Other crowns and protectorates",html:"<p>Albania retains a separate crown and ministries; Ethiopia combines imperial title, viceregal government, and local institutions; Egypt remains a protectorate monarchy; and Syria develops as an associated Arab kingdom with Italian bases and advisers.</p>"},
      {id:"church",title:"Ecclesial and dynastic plurality",html:"<p>Catholics, Orthodox, Copts, Syriacs, Ethiopian Christians, and Muslims inhabit the same Italian-led order. The monarchy and later council doctrine treat separate patrimonies as compatible with one strategic crown system without claiming one Italian race or church.</p>"},
      {id:"consequence",title:"The future constitutional problem",html:"<p>Every ministry and assembly created to reduce occupation costs becomes a possible advocate of sovereignty. The opening preserves Italian supremacy in the short term by creating Greek, Yugoslav, and associated institutions capable of contesting it in the long term.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"italian-union-of-crowns.html",kicker:"Constitutional system",label:"Italian Union of Crowns"},{href:"act-of-crown-association-greece.html",kicker:"Greek instrument",label:"Act of Crown Association"},{href:"federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html",kicker:"Federal creation",label:"Federal Yugoslavia"},{href:"italy.html",kicker:"Imperial center",label:"Kingdom of Italy"}],
    facts:[["Period","1960–1962"],["Principal ruler","Umberto II"],["Immediate context","Mussolini's death and end of the Duce title"],["Method","Separate crowns and delegated domestic government"],["Reserved powers","Defense, foreign policy, bases, customs, and strategic finance"],["Principal creations","Separate Greece and Federal Yugoslavia"],["Official claim","Association and permanent order"],["Analytical character","Indirect imperial consolidation"]]
  }),

  "italian-union-of-crowns":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Italian Union of Crowns and Associated Kingdoms",eyebrow:"Composite imperial constitution · c. 1962 onward",infoboxKicker:"Italian-led dynastic system",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/italian-union-of-crowns.svg",alt:"Diagram of the Italian union of crowns",caption:"Domestic plurality was organized beneath common Italian reserved powers"},
    lead:"The Italian Union of Crowns and Associated Kingdoms is the conventional name for the legally differentiated imperial order centered on Umberto II. Italy, Albania, Ethiopia, Greece, Federal Yugoslavia, Egypt, Syria, and other dependencies do not form one federal state; they possess different crowns and governments joined by Italian defense, finance, customs, bases, and foreign alignment.",
    canon:"The union-of-crowns model, its distinction from federal Italy, separate Greek and Yugoslav realms, Albanian and Ethiopian titles, Egyptian and Syrian protectorate layers, and Italian strategic reserve are established. Whether the system has one formal collective title and how its crowns pass after Umberto remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"precedent",title:"Savoyard precedent",html:"<p>The House of Savoy already claims several legally distinct realms. Victory preserves the Albanian kingship and Ethiopian imperial title, allowing Umberto to describe later crowns as associated sovereignties rather than provinces of metropolitan Italy.</p>"},
      {id:"not-federation",title:"Not federal Italy",html:"<p>The system has no single citizenship, parliament, civil code, church, or equal territorial chamber. Each realm has its own legal basis. What joins them is the crown's person and a series of treaties reserving strategy to Rome.</p>"},
      {id:"layers",title:"The constitutional layers",html:"<p>Italy retains core sovereignty. Albania has separate ministries; Ethiopia uses viceregal and local institutions; Greece and Yugoslavia possess conquest crowns and cabinets; Egypt is a protectorate monarchy; Syria is an associated Arab kingdom. Similar words conceal different degrees of autonomy.</p>"},
      {id:"reserve",title:"The Italian strategic reserve",html:"<p>No associated realm may wage war, leave the economic system, change alignment, deny agreed bases, or control strategic finance against Rome. External sovereignty is therefore narrower than the domestic apparatus suggests.</p>"},
      {id:"crown",title:"The person of Umberto II",html:"<p>Umberto's accumulated crowns provide a common arbiter where no one bureaucracy could plausibly govern all peoples directly. The arrangement is personal enough that succession becomes its greatest unsolved constitutional question.</p>"},
      {id:"religion",title:"Several peoples and churches",html:"<p>Latin, Eastern Catholic, Orthodox, Coptic, Syriac, Ethiopian, and Muslim institutions require differentiated settlement. Umberto uses royal protection and ecclesial patrimony to present plurality as ordered inheritance rather than a temporary defect awaiting Italianization.</p>"},
      {id:"church-diplomacy",title:"Church diplomacy and the crowns",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-j9r4k writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:07:44-06:00 --><p>The personal union created no imperial church. Greece retained an Orthodox establishment. Ethiopian and Coptic churches remained governed through their own hierarchies. Eastern Catholic churches preserved distinct patriarchal or episcopal government, and Latin Catholic dioceses and religious institutes remained within Catholic communion. The king's protection, a ministry's license, or an imperial subsidy had civil consequences in each realm but did not create episcopal succession, sacramental jurisdiction, or communion with Rome.</p><p>Holy See diplomacy therefore followed the legal layer involved. A concordat or diplomatic exchange with one crown regulated the subjects named in that instrument and did not automatically bind every associated kingdom or any Orthodox church that was not a party. Catholic bishops, Eastern synods, Orthodox hierarchies, religious superiors, and civil ministries could cooperate over schools, hospitals, missions, marriage effects, property, visas, and chaplaincies while retaining different sources of authority. Clergy and religious houses operating across crown frontiers remained subject both to their competent ecclesiastical superiors and to the applicable civil law.</p><p>Umbertian language of royal protection and ecclesial patrimony overlapped with the later Council of Christ the King, but the council did not make the composite monarchy a doctrinal model. Religious immunity excluded coerced profession, and ecclesial patrimony protected churches and peoples from forced Italianization while leaving reunion and evangelization as ecclesiastical questions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Treaties, concordats, church statutes, appointment and property records, subsidy conditions, police orders, and testimony from clergy, monastics, women religious, teachers, patients, pupils, and religious minorities are required to distinguish protection from political capture.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Economic coordination without one state",html:"<p>Customs preferences, shipping, concessions, currencies, Scudo clearing, corporate investment, and development finance bind the realms to Italy and the Latin Community. Domestic treasuries exist, but the strategic direction of capital remains external.</p>"},
      {id:"succession",title:"The unresolved succession",html:"<p>Some crowns may pass with the House of Savoy; the Greek crown is designed for Umberto's lifetime; Yugoslav succession is unsettled; protectorate treaties can name different mechanisms. A peaceful transition must decide whether one heir receives a system or each realm receives a choice.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"umbertian-opening.html",kicker:"Founding reform",label:"Umbertian Opening"},{href:"act-of-crown-association-greece.html",kicker:"Greek crown",label:"Act of Crown Association"},{href:"federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html",kicker:"Yugoslav crown",label:"Federal Yugoslavia"},{href:"council-of-christ-the-king.html",kicker:"Ecclesial doctrine",label:"Council of Christ the King"}],
    facts:[["Center","Rome"],["Principal sovereign","Umberto II"],["Core state","Kingdom of Italy"],["Separate crowns","Albania, Ethiopia, Greece, and Federal Yugoslavia"],["Protected or associated realms","Egypt and Syria"],["Common reserve","Defense, foreign alignment, bases, customs, and strategic finance"],["Constitutional form","Personal and treaty union, not federation"],["Collective title and succession","Open"]]
  }),

  "act-of-crown-association-greece":balkanOrderArticle({
    title:"Act of Crown Association for Greece",eyebrow:"Greek constitutional settlement · c. 1961–1962",infoboxKicker:"Imperial association instrument",
    flag:{src:"assets/flags/greece.svg",alt:"Blue and white flag of Greece",caption:"Greek national flag retained after separate statehood was restored"},
    lead:"The Act of Crown Association for Greece was the constitutional instrument that ended Greece's legal incorporation into the Italian empire and restored a separate kingdom beneath Umberto II. It returned Greek ministries, courts, treasury, language, armed forces, and Orthodox establishment while reserving defense, treaties, customs alignment, bases, and upper command to Italy.",
    canon:"A separate Greek kingdom around 1961–62, Umberto as King of Greece rather than King of the Hellenes, Greek domestic institutions, Italian strategic reserve, a lifetime crown, and open later succession are established as the preferred working settlement. The act's final name, exact date, and clauses remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"occupation",title:"From conquest to association",html:"<p>Italy's wartime conquest could hold ports and cities but could not indefinitely administer Greek society without immense cost. Separate statehood turns local officials from tolerated instruments of occupation into responsible administrators beneath an unchanged strategic hierarchy.</p>"},
      {id:"dynasty",title:"A conquest crown",html:"<p>Greece already possesses an exiled national dynasty. Umberto therefore does not fill a vacant throne; he creates a new crown grounded in conquest and association. The title King of Greece avoids the stronger national claim implied by King of the Hellenes.</p>"},
      {id:"domestic",title:"Restored domestic institutions",html:"<p>Greece receives a cabinet, treasury, courts, civil service, provincial government, flag, and armed formations. Greek remains the language of administration, and ordinary law is no longer presented as an extension of Italian provincial rule.</p>"},
      {id:"church",title:"The Orthodox establishment",html:"<p>The Orthodox Church receives constitutional recognition and protection. This gives the crown a local institution through which ceremony, education, charity, and public morality can operate without requiring Catholic or Italian assimilation.</p>"},
      {id:"ecclesiastical-effect",title:"Civil recognition and ecclesiastical authority",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-h6q2m writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:07:44-06:00 --><p>The act's recognition of the Orthodox establishment concerned civil status: legal personality, property, schools, charities, public ceremonies, chaplaincies, and the effects assigned to ecclesiastical marriage. It did not create Orthodox orders or jurisdiction. Any royal or ministerial approval required for a senior appointment determined public recognition and access to the offices protected by the state; the ecclesiastical act itself depended upon the competent Orthodox authority. The Catholic sovereign possessed no episcopal office by virtue of the Greek crown.</p><p>Protection for Catholic missions and Eastern Catholic communities likewise did not place them under the Orthodox establishment or authorize Italian officials to direct Catholic appointments and sacraments. Their civil operation depended upon Greek law and the association settlement, while their clergy and institutions remained governed by the applicable Catholic ordinary, Eastern hierarchy, religious superior, and universal or particular church law. The Holy See's hope for reunion supplied no constitutional warrant for compulsory conversion or a state-authored union.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The exact text and date of the act remain open, including any appointment, education, property, marriage, minority-protection, or mission clauses. Its operation must therefore be reconstructed from the final instrument and implementing regulations, royal and ministerial files, synodal and episcopal acts, police correspondence, court cases, budgets, school inspections, and petitions from parishes, monasteries, missions, families, and minorities. Later Catholic–Orthodox agreements required acts by the competent churches and did not retroactively transform the crown settlement into ecclesial reunion.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"reserve",title:"Italian reserved powers",html:"<p>Rome retains principal naval bases, external defense, treaty authority, customs alignment, and upper military command. The drachma survives inside a fixed Italian clearing arrangement rather than becoming a fully independent currency.</p>"},
      {id:"lifetime",title:"A crown for Umberto's lifetime",html:"<p>Automatic Savoyard inheritance would make association look like annexation under another name. A lifetime crown leaves later succession to a Greek Crown Council and treaty settlement, preserving the possibility of negotiation with a Greek dynast.</p>"},
      {id:"nationalism",title:"Administration without reconciliation",html:"<p>The act changes the nationalist complaint from abolition of Greece to rule by a foreign king. It reduces garrison requirements and creates meaningful offices while leaving resistance, dynastic loyalty, territorial grievance, and demands for genuine sovereignty alive.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Model and warning",html:"<p>The Greek arrangement becomes a model for the <a href='federal-kingdom-yugoslavia.html'>Yugoslav federal crown</a> and the wider union of crowns. It also demonstrates the system's central risk: institutions built to administer dependence can acquire the language and personnel of independence.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"greece.html",kicker:"Country profile",label:"Kingdom of Greece"},{href:"umbertian-opening.html",kicker:"Reform context",label:"Umbertian Opening"},{href:"italian-union-of-crowns.html",kicker:"Composite system",label:"Italian Union of Crowns"},{href:"italy.html",kicker:"Strategic sovereign",label:"Kingdom of Italy"}],
    facts:[["Working date","c. 1961–1962"],["Associated sovereign","Umberto II"],["Royal style","King of Greece"],["Domestic restoration","Cabinet, courts, treasury, civil service, and forces"],["Established church","Greek Orthodox Church"],["Currency","Drachma in fixed Italian clearing"],["Italian reserve","Defense, treaties, bases, customs, and upper command"],["Succession","Greek Crown Council and later treaty; open"]]
  })
});

const addBalkanRelated=(slug,items)=>{
  const article=window.deepArticles[slug];if(!article)return;article.related=article.related||[];
  for(const item of items)if(!article.related.some(existing=>existing.href===item.href))article.related.unshift(item);
};

